  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 PM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe regional radar mosaic shows multiple areas of convection \nupstream over the Ohio Valley and southern Great Lakes. The exact \nevolution of these structures is uncertain, but the likelihood of \none of these structures to impact our area is high. There are a few \nisolated thunderstorms developing across the southwest VA ahead of \nthe main MCS over N KY and W WV. These storms could produce isolated \nwinds and heavy rainfall. If we see repeated rounds of storms and \nshowers over any area, the potential for flooding and flash flooding \nwill increase and may require flood headlines in the future. \nForecast soundings across SW VA & NE TN highlight 2000-3000+ j/kg \nCAPE with ~20 knots of shear. This is plenty for clusters of \nthunderstorms to develop and produce isolated hail/wind damage. \nForecast soundings further west in SE TN highlight significant \ninstabilty available for thunderstorms to tap into. The general \nconsensus is that we'll see the MCS or a portion of the MCS upstream \nfollow the instability gradient that goes through our eastern \nforecast area of SW VA/NE TN. The instability in place will be \nplenty for MCS maintenance and the structure itself will likely be \nable to enhance the local shear environment to a more favorable \nsituation. The timing appears to be between 4 to 8pm for our NE area \nwith uncertainty on how far south/west it'll continue. Additional \nMCS over central Indiana should continue to move south-southeast and \nshould arrive in our northern area this evening. The exact evolution \nof this is even more uncertain due to the unknowns with this \nafternoon's potential convection. There is reason to believe that \nthe second round should maintain some intensity and bring another \nround of thunderstorms capable of producing damaging winds and heavy \nrain. This structure does have some mid level support as an impulse \ntransits through the Ohio Valley this evening/tonight. This should \nmove through the area this evening and into tonight before we begin \nto dry out. Given the general uncertainty and lower-than-normal \nconfidence with this forecast, please monitor the forecast if you \nhave any outdoor work or activities planned this afternoon - evening.\n\nWednesday through Friday... this period remains unsettled with the \npotential for additional showers and thunderstorms each afternoon. A \nstrong ridge remains off to our west and a trough to our east over \nthe NE CONUS. This places us in a favorable area for daily \nshower/thunderstorm chances through Friday evening. There will be a \nnon-zero chance for a few of these storm to become strong to severe \nand able to produce heavy rainfall. There isn't a clear signal for \nany one area but rather a broad brush approach given the unstable \nairmass and northwest flow aloft. The finer details won't be \nresolved likely until the day of given the lower confidence tied to \nthis pattern. \n\nSaturday through Early Next Week... The western ridge axis will \nbuild into the area this weekend. This will allow warmer \ntemperatures to settle in through the start of next week. \nPrecipitation chances will continue through the weekend, but the \nchance of any organized convection will be much lower. If the ridge \nbuilds in further east, we may even stay mostly dry Saturday/Sunday, \nwhich would welcomed.",
